---
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import Explorer from '../components/Explorer.astro';
import { project } from '../project.config';
---

<Project>
	<h1>{project.title}</h1>
	<p class="lead">{project.summary}</p>

	<h2>The question</h2>
	<p>Which US states gained or lost population between 2010 and 2025, and by how much?</p>

	<h2>The short answer</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The US grew 10.5%, from 309.3 million to 341.8 million people.</li>
		<li>Idaho (+29.2%), Utah (+27.5%), Texas (+25.6%) and Florida (+24.5%) grew fastest.</li>
		<li>Texas and Florida alone added 11.1 million people, about a third of the net national gain.</li>
		<li>California is still the largest state, but at +5.5% it grew about half as fast as the country.</li>
		<li>Only three states shrank: West Virginia (&minus;4.8%), Illinois (about &minus;1%) and Mississippi (about &minus;0.5%).</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Explore it</h2>
	<p>
		Move the year slider, or press Play, to see the map and the ranking change. Pick states to compare in the trend chart. The table at the
		bottom has every number.
	</p>
	<Explorer />

	<h2>How I got these numbers</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>Downloaded two official files from the US Census Bureau (see the table below), on 2026-10-29.</li>
		<li>Kept the 50 states and the District of Columbia. Dropped Puerto Rico, which the Census does not count in the US total.</li>
		<li>Used the 2010&ndash;2019 release for 2010&ndash;2019, and the 2020&ndash;2025 release for 2020&ndash;2025. All figures are July 1 estimates.</li>
		<li>Calculated growth as each year's population divided by the 2010 population, minus one.</li>
		<li>Checked that the 51 state figures add up to the Census's own US total, in all 16 years. They do.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>Limits</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>The two releases do not line up.</strong> The 2020&ndash;2025 estimates start from the 2020 Census count. The US total in that release
			begins at 331.5 million, 1.0% above the older release's 2019 estimate of 328.2 million. For individual states the gap runs from +4.6% (New
			Jersey) to &minus;2.3% (District of Columbia). A change across the dashed line is partly a method change, not only people moving. Treat small
			gaps between states with care.
		</li>
		<li>These are estimates, not head counts. The Census revises them with each release.</li>
		<li>The page shows how many people live in each state, not why. It does not separate births, deaths and moves between states.</li>
		<li>The map outlines are simplified for display, so small borders are approximate. Alaska's Aleutian Islands are shifted to keep the state in one piece.</li>
		<li>The map stays light-colored when your device is in dark mode.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Data</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap-static">
		<table class="sources">
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Source</th><th>Retrieved</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>State population estimates, 2010&ndash;2019</td>
					<td>
						US Census Bureau,
						<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2010-2019/national/totals/nst-est2019-alldata.csv">nst-est2019-alldata.csv</a>
					</td>
					<td>2026-10-29</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>State population estimates, 2020&ndash;2025</td>
					<td>
						US Census Bureau,
						<a href="https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2025/state/totals/NST-EST2025-ALLDATA.csv">NST-EST2025-ALLDATA.csv</a>
					</td>
					<td>2026-10-29</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>State outlines (1:20,000,000)</td>
					<td>
						US Census Bureau,
						<a href="https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2024/shp/cb_2024_us_state_20m.zip">cb_2024_us_state_20m.zip</a>
					</td>
					<td>2026-10-29</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Cleaned files are in the repository under <code>data/processed/</code>. Basemap: OpenFreeMap, &copy; OpenMapTiles, data from OpenStreetMap.
	</p>
</Project>

<style is:global>
	.table-wrap-static {
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
	table.sources {
		border-collapse: collapse;
		width: 100%;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}
	table.sources th,
	table.sources td {
		text-align: left;
		padding: 0.4rem 0.7rem 0.4rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
		vertical-align: top;
	}
</style>
